---
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
---

<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="UTF-8" />
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
		<link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="256x256" href="/favicon-original-tent-close.png?v=8" />
		<link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png?v=8" />
		<title>What do your parents think? — Olivia Fjord Sloth</title>
		<meta name="description" content="A personal essay about adventure, family and the parts of ourselves we are expected to outgrow." />
	</head>

	<body>
		<SiteHeader />

		<main>
			<section class="hero">
				<img src="/stories/hama2.webp" alt="Olivia Fjord Sloth in the mountains at sunset" decoding="async" />

				<div class="hero-text">
					<p class="category">PERSONAL ESSAY</p>

					<h1>What do your<br />parents think?</h1>

					<p class="subtitle">
						About adventure, family and the parts of ourselves
						we’re expected to outgrow
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>
			<article>
				<p class="lead">
					One of the questions I’ve been asked most since sharing that I’m leaving my job and going to Syria is:
				</p>

				<p>
					<strong>“What do your parents think?”</strong>
				</p>

				<p>
					I always find that question a little strange.
				</p>

				<p>
					I’m a grown woman, and I sometimes wonder whether people would ask a man the same thing.
				</p>

				<p>
					But I understand why people are curious.
				</p>

				<p>
					My mom and I have always had very different ideas of what a good life looks like. She values stability, security and a life that doesn’t have to be more difficult than necessary.
				</p>

				<p>
					I seem to do the opposite.
				</p>

				<p>
					I deliberately put myself in situations that challenge the way I think. Sometimes that means travelling alone to places most people wouldn’t choose. Other times, it means turning my PhD defence into a story about a little prince who wants to save the world.
				</p>

				<p>
					The easy and comfortable path has never really felt like an option to me.
				</p>

				<p>
					That doesn’t mean my choices have always worked out well. I’ve fallen flat on my face more than once. And sometimes I look at my mother and think: maybe she has a point.
				</p>

				<p>
					Why do I keep choosing things that are difficult?
				</p>

				<p>
					I don’t have a particularly good answer.
				</p>

				<p>
					I have a stable job. I earn a good salary. I can go home before 4am and leave work behind.
				</p>

				<p>
					And I’m not thriving in it.
				</p>

				<p>
					My dad is my biggest fan. But he also sometimes puts his hand on my shoulder and says something along the lines of:
				</p>

				<blockquote>
					“I really hope you find a man, have some children and build a life where you don’t always need to climb mountains.”
				</blockquote>

				<p>
					Not necessarily literal mountains.
				</p>

				<p>
					That one hits harder, because I want those things too.
				</p>

				<p>
					My biggest fear about leaving my job wasn’t Syria.
				</p>

				<p>
					It was that I might be moving further away from having children.
				</p>

				<p>
					I’m turning 35 soon, and I dream of having a family one day.
				</p>

				<p>
					For years, every time I planned another trip, I told myself:
				</p>

				<blockquote>
					This will be the last one. Then I’ll start building a more conventional life.
				</blockquote>

				<p>
					I would return to dating and try to become a slightly more sensible version of myself.
				</p>

				<p>
					When I spoke about the adventurous part of my life, I often added:
				</p>

				<blockquote>
					“Of course, I would never do this if I had children.”
				</blockquote>

				<p>
					I think I said it because I was afraid that this part of me didn’t look like good “mother material.”
				</p>

				<p>
					For a long time, I treated it as something temporary, partly because people often spoke about it as if it were something I would eventually grow out of.
				</p>

				<p>
					One last trip.<br />
					One last mountain.<br />
					One last adventure.
				</p>
				<figure class="story-image">
					<img src="/stories/rock.webp" alt="Hiking in the mountains" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
				</figure>
				<p>
					And then I would become serious about the life I was supposed to want.
				</p>

				<p>
					But I don’t think this is something I need to grow out of.
				</p>

				<p>
					It isn’t a phase, and it isn’t a part of myself I want to hide.
				</p>

				<p>
					I’m taking the adventure seriously now.
				</p>

				<p>
					Not because I have stopped dreaming of love or children. I haven’t.
				</p>

				<p>
					But if I never get the family I dream of, and I also spend these years abandoning the life I want to live, then I risk losing both.
				</p>

				<p>
					I still hope there is room for both.
				</p>

				<p>
					I just can’t keep putting one dream on hold while waiting for the other.
				</p>
			</article>

			<div class="back">
				<a href="/stories">← Back to stories</a>
			</div>
		</main>
		<SiteFooter />
	</body>
</html>


<style>
	* {
		box-sizing: border-box;
	}

	html,
	body {
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
		background: #f4f1ea;
		color: #171717;
	}

	body {
		font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
	}

	header {
		width: 100%;
		padding: 30px 48px;
		display: flex;
		justify-content: space-between;
		align-items: center;
	}

	a {
		color: inherit;
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	.name {
		font-size: 22px;
		font-weight: 700;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
	}

	nav {
		display: flex;
		gap: 32px;
	}

	nav a {
		font-size: 15px;
	}

	.story-header {
		max-width: 1050px;
		margin: 110px auto 80px;
		padding: 0 48px;
	}
	.story-image {
		margin: 55px 0;
		width: 100%;
	}

	.story-image img {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
	}
	.category {
		margin: 0 0 25px;
		font-size: 12px;
		letter-spacing: 0.18em;
	}

	h1 {
		margin: 0;
		font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
		font-size: clamp(58px, 8vw, 115px);
		font-weight: 400;
		line-height: 0.95;
		letter-spacing: -0.035em;
	}

	.subtitle {
		max-width: 750px;
		margin: 35px 0 0;
		font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
		font-size: clamp(23px, 2.5vw, 32px);
		line-height: 1.4;
		color: #666;
	}

	.hero {
		position: relative;
		width: 100%;
		height: min(78vh, 850px);
		overflow: hidden;
	}

	.hero img {
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;
		object-fit: cover;
	}

	.hero::after {
		content: "";
		position: absolute;
		inset: 0;
		background: linear-gradient(
			90deg,
			rgba(0,0,0,0.38) 0%,
			rgba(0,0,0,0.10) 60%,
			rgba(0,0,0,0) 100%
		);
	}

	.hero-text {
		position: absolute;
		z-index: 2;
		left: 8%;
		top: 25%;
		transform: translateY(-50%);
		max-width: 850px;
		color: #f4f1ea;
	}

	.hero-text h1 {
		margin: 15px 0 25px;
		font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
		font-size: clamp(60px, 7vw, 120px);
		font-weight: 400;
		line-height: 0.9;
		letter-spacing: -0.04em;
	}

	.hero-text .subtitle {
		max-width: 650px;
		margin: 0;
		font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
		font-size: clamp(20px, 2vw, 30px);
		line-height: 1.3;
	}

	.category {
		font-size: 12px;
		letter-spacing: 0.18em;
	}

	article {
		max-width: 700px;
		margin: 90px auto 0;
		padding: 0 30px;

		font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
		font-size: 21px;
		line-height: 1.75;
	}

	article p {
		margin: 0 0 34px;
	}

	.lead {
		font-size: 27px;
		line-height: 1.5;
	}

	.back {
		max-width: 700px;
		margin: 90px auto 120px;
		padding: 35px 30px 0;
		border-top: 1px solid #bbb;
		font-size: 14px;
	}

	@media (max-width: 700px) {
		header {
			padding: 24px 20px;
		}

		.name {
			font-size: 17px;
		}

		nav {
			gap: 14px;
		}

		nav a {
			font-size: 13px;
		}

		.story-header {
			margin: 65px auto 55px;
			padding: 0 24px;
		}

		h1 {
			font-size: 58px;
		}

		.subtitle {
			font-size: 25px;
		}

		article {
			margin-top: 60px;
			padding: 0 24px;
			font-size: 19px;
		}

		.lead {
			font-size: 23px;
		}

		.back {
			margin-top: 70px;
			padding-left: 24px;
			padding-right: 24px;
		}
	}
</style>
